  </data>
  <data name="GlobalHotkeysCheckBox.ToolTipService.ToolTip" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Also while you are working in another app. On Ctrl+Alt rather than Ctrl, because claiming Ctrl+Space for this app would take it away from every other one. A combination another app already holds is left to it and named below.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="GlobalHotkeysText.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Ctrl+Alt+P to stop or play, Ctrl+Alt+M to mute, Ctrl+Alt+→ and Ctrl+Alt+← for the next and previous favorite, from whatever app you are working in.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="LoudnessHeading.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Loudness</value>
  </data>
  <data name="LoudnessText.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>One station is mastered several decibels louder than the next, which is the most annoying part of zapping. The audio of every favorite is decoded anyway, so how loud its music really is is measured from it (EBU R128), and the loud ones are turned down to the same level. Only music counts, so ads and presenters do not skew it, and it takes about a minute of music per station. Stations that cannot be listened to (HLS) keep the volume they come in at.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="NormalizeLoudnessCheckBox.Content" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Match the loudness of stations</value>
  </data>
  <data name="NormalizeLoudnessCheckBox.ToolTipService.ToolTip" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Brings every station to about -14 LUFS, the level streaming services normalize to, by at most 12 dB down or 6 dB up. A station that has to go up cannot pass full volume, so with the slider at the top it may stay a little quieter.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="NoFavoritesLoudnessText.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>No favorites yet.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="RemeasureLoudnessButton.Content" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Measure loudness again</value>
  </data>
  <data name="RemeasureLoudnessButton.ToolTipService.ToolTip" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Forgets what was heard of every favorite so far and measures it again. The volume keeps the current correction until about a minute of music has been heard, and then moves to the new result.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="CacheHeading.Text" xml:space="preserve">
