  <data name="CrossfadeHeading.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Crossfade</value>
  </data>
  <data name="CrossfadeText.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Fades from one station into the other instead of cutting over. The fade out ends before the break starts, so no ad leaks in. A station that plays live instead of from the time-shift buffer is still cut off, because its break can already be heard.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="CrossfadeCheckBox.Content" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Crossfade when zapping</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ZapTargetsHeading.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Zap to</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ZapTargetsText.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The favorites a break may be zapped to. Leave out a station that is no place to wait for the music, such as a news station; it is still zapped away from and returned to.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="NoZapTargetsText.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>No favorites yet. Add stations to your favorites to choose where the zapper may go.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ZapToSongStartHeading.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Zap to the start of the song</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ZapToSongStartText.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A zap lands on a station that is playing a song, usually halfway through it. To start that song from its beginning instead, the last minutes of every favorite are kept in memory (a time-shift buffer). It also cuts a break off exactly where it begins, because the break is known before it is heard. You then listen a little behind the broadcast, at most the length of the song a zap landed in, and Go live catches up at once. Switched off, no buffers are kept and their memory is freed: every station plays live, and a zap lands wherever the song is.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ZapToSongStartCheckBox.Content" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Start the song from its beginning when zapping</value>
  </data>
  <data name="TimeShiftMemory" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Uses about {0} MB of memory for your {1} favorites. Keeping fewer minutes, or switching this off, saves memory.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="TimeShiftMemoryOff" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Off, so it uses no memory. Switched on, it would take about {0} MB for your {1} favorites.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="TimeShiftMemoryPerFavorite" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Takes about {0} MB of memory per favorite, for a station at 128 kbit/s.</value>
  </data>
